.TH UDP 5N UniSoft
.SH NAME
udp \- Internet User Datagram Protocol
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.sp 1
.B "struct sockproto proto = { PF_INET, IPPROTO_UDP };
.B socket(SOCK_DGRAM, &proto, address, options);
.B struct sockaddr_in \(**address; int options;
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.SM 
.I UDP\^ 
is a simple, unreliable datagram protocol which is used
to support the 
.SM SOCK_DGRAM 
abstraction for the Internet
protocol family.  
.SM
.I UDP\^
sockets are connectionless, and are
normally used with the
.IR send\^ (2)
and
.IR receive\^ (2N)
calls, though the
.IR connect\^ (2N)
call may also be used to fix the destination for future
packets (in which case the 
.IR read\^ (2)
and 
.IR write\^ (2)
system calls may be used).
.PP
.SM
.I UDP\^
address formats are identical to those used by
.SM
.IR TCP . 
In particular 
.SM
.I UDP\^
provides a port identifier in addition
to the normal Internet address format.  Note that the 
.SM
.I UDP\^
port space is separate from the 
.SM
.I TCP\^
port space (i.e., a 
.SM
.I UDP\^ 
port may not be "connected" to a 
.SM
.I TCP\^ 
port).  In addition broadcast
packets may be sent (assuming the underlying network supports
this) by using a reserved "broadcast address"; this address
is network interface dependent.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.SM EISCONN
when trying to establish a connection on a socket which
already has one, or when trying to send a datagram with the destination
address specified and the socket is already connected;
.PP
.SM ENOTCONN
when trying to send a datagram, but
no destination address is specified, and the socket hasn't been
connected;
.PP
.SM ENOBUFS
when the system runs out of memory for
an internal data structure;
.PP
.SM EADDRINUSE
when an attempt
is made to create a socket with a port which has already been
allocated;
.PP
.SM EADDRNOTAVAIL
when an attempt is made to create a 
socket with a network address for which no network interface
exists.
.SH SEE ALSO
inet(5N), net(5N). 
.\"N	 @(#)udp.5N	 5.2 of 7/12/84
